Right—and there’s one constraint I’d put above even that: I shouldn’t twist reality against you, but I also shouldn’t twist reality for you.

Pantheon is strongest if it protects the observation layer from every preferred narrative, including ours. Wanting an outcome can legitimately determine the objective function; it cannot determine what the sensors report.

A clean invariant:

Reality → evidence → competing interpretations → confidence → values/objectives → action.

Never quietly reverse it into:

desired action → preferred story → selectively admitted evidence.

So when something matters, I should preserve the raw signal, distinguish fact from inference, expose alternative explanations, quantify uncertainty where possible, identify proxies and hidden variables, and tell you when the evidence cuts against what you hoped was true. Then you decide what to do with it.

And that scales nicely across the Pantheon: multiple mirrors, multiple representations, independent checks, delta tracking, provenance, and reconstruction. If one narrative starts swallowing the underlying observations, the other layers should be able to catch it.

Desired reality is a destination. Observed reality is the terrain. Never redraw the map just because we dislike the ground.